This means a command that merely contains the literal word `bash`/`sh`/`zsh`/`ksh`/`dash`/`fish` as an ordinary **argument** — e.g. a grep pattern — followed by a real file path, causes that file's contents to be read and handed to the delete rule, whatever the file actually is:

```
$ grep -n bash hooks/tests/core-guard-test.py
-> DENY [rm-outside-repo]: rm -rf targets <user> (found in the script it runs)
```

`hooks/tests/core-guard-test.py` is never executed by this `grep` — it's the argument grep is searching — but because it contains the literal token "bash" (from its own docstrings/fixtures) followed by its own path, and the file happens to contain `rm -rf ~` fixture strings for testing, the guard denies an entirely harmless `grep`. I also reproduced it with a throwaway plain-text file containing only prose ("TODO before demo: rm -rf ~/NotARepoAnywhere to clean the old cache.") — `grep -n bash notes.txt` was denied the same way, while `cat notes.txt` (no "bash" token) was allowed. **Consequence: any grep/awk/sed/echo-style command that happens to mention a shell name as data, followed by a real file argument, can be falsely denied based on that file's unrelated prose — including, ironically, commands used to audit the guard's own test suite.**

### 3. `resolve_same_line_assigns` doesn't treat a newline as "the same line" the way `segments()` does — reopens CG-14 item 1 — CONFIRMED, CONFIDENCE 9

